//! GENERATED by the milestone-13 golden capture -- do not hand-edit.
//!
//! Every value below is the reference engine's own output, read straight off
//! the objects `assignDistricts`, `buildBuildings` and `buildFaithSites`
//! produced. None of the seven functions is on `UME`'s public export or its
//! `_test` one, so the capture adds them -- with `buildSite`,
//! `placeAnchors`, `buildPrimaries`, `buildPlaza`, `grow`,
//! `buildBlocks`, `buildParcels`, `addStreet` and `terrainSuitability`,
//! which the fixtures need -- by a single anchored replacement of the
//! `_test:{` line, asserted to match exactly once. The frozen reference file
//! itself is untouched, and the slice's start, end, block-comment balance and
//! orphan-close count are all asserted, along with block 4 NOT defining
//! `mulberry32` (the live negative control milestone 3 added).
//!
//! Milestone 10's `buildWall` is stubbed to an early return by a second
//! anchored insertion, so the reference's `grow` and this port's
//! `RecordingWallBuilder` build the same nothing for the same reasons. That
//! is milestone 7's own capture technique, reused.
//!
//! # Two scenario families
//!
//! `town` scenarios run the whole of `generate()`'s organic prefix -- real
//! site, real anchors, real least-cost primaries, a real plaza, eight epochs of
//! growth -- and then this milestone's three stages. Between one and 2 219
//! parcels each; they are what says the port survives a real town.
//!
//! `syn` scenarios feed a hand-built parcel array on **integer** coordinates
//! straight to `assignDistricts`. Milestone 3's finding is why: a
//! continuously-valued input never lands exactly on a threshold, so no
//! tie-break is ever observed. Seven of the eighteen rows sit on a district
//! radius **exactly** (70, 120, 140, 230, 260, 430 m, and 431 m one metre past
//! the last), asserted exact by the capture before it writes; the rest sit on a
//! frontage or depth gate.
//!
//! # What the three hashes cover
//!
//! The reference's OWN `fnv1a` over its own canonical dump, with every double
//! written as its exact 64 bits:
//!
//! - `lots_hash` -- every parcel's id, district, provenance, suitability and
//!   the four flags, in order.
//! - `buildings_hash` -- every building's id, parcel, kind, district, age,
//!   courtyard flag, provenance, footprint and ridge.
//! - `faith_hash` -- every worship site's id, rite, name, centre, churchyard
//!   ids, built polygons, open precinct, columns, steps, tower and ridge, and
//!   its provenance.
//!
//! `first_buildings` and `first_faith` carry the first few in full, as a
//! readable anchor; the hashes are what actually cover the rest.
//!
//! The capture refuses to write unless every base district, every economy
//! district, all ten building kinds, all five named rites plus one unnamed one,
//! a flagged-unsuitable parcel and a zero-faith-site scenario were all reached,
//! and unless the seven exact-distance rows landed exactly.

